  "query_preview": "..A shortwave trough which brought several rounds of\nshowers and thunderstorms across northern Utah today is quickly\nlifting north through the northern Rockies. A few lingering\nshowers persist across far northern Utah in proximity to the\ntrailing trough axis, however a cooler and more stable airmass in\nthe wake of this trough is settling into the forecast area, which\nhas largely brought an end to convection across the CWA. This\nairmass will reside across the area Wednesday resulting in\nseasonal temperatures and dry conditions. \n\nA lingering deformation axis will help pull moisture back into\nsouthern Utah Thursday resulting in a chance of showers and\nthunderstorms mainly over the higher terrain. Otherwise mid level\nridging will bring a warming trend across the forecast area, which\nwill persist through Friday. A transition to a dry southwesterly\nflow aloft will follow for the upcoming weekend.\n\nThe next trough of note is forecast to swing through the region\nearly next week. Considerable uncertainty remains regarding \ntiming and amplitude of this trough, in addition to whether its \nable to tap into late season monsoon moisture, which may be \nenhanced by eastern Pacific tropical activity. The most recent \ncluster analysis shows a little less than half of solutions, \nlargely dominated by GFS and Canadian members, pull this moisture\ninto the forecast area Monday, while 56% of solutions, heavily \ninfluenced by ECMWF members, maintain a drier airmass across the \nregion.",
